    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>How does performance-based hiring work for students?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Performance-based hiring judges you on what you can produce, not where you studied. It is now the mainstream approach: <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers used skills-based hiring in 2025</a>. For a student with no experience, that is good news, because you can compete on demonstrated skill instead of pedigree, as long as you make the skill visible.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers used skills-based hiring in 2025</a>, up from 56% in 2022, per TestGorilla.</li>
        <li>Résumé reliance is falling: 67% of employers used résumés in 2025, down from 73% the year before (same report).</li>
        <li>Be honest about the gap: adoption is often claimed more than fully practiced, so this is a strong direction rather than a finished revolution.</li>
        <li>For students, it removes pedigree as the gate and rewards demonstrated ability, which is the one place effort can beat credentials.</li>
        <li>You prepare by producing scored work in your target role, so the evidence exists before an employer asks for it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What is performance-based hiring?</h2>
      <p>Performance-based hiring, often called skills-based hiring, tries to predict how well you will do a job from evidence that you can actually do it. Instead of reading your degree and inferring competence, an employer asks you to demonstrate the skill directly, through a work sample, a skills test, a take-home task, or a structured exercise that mirrors the real work. The credential becomes one input among several rather than the deciding filter.</p>
      <p>The shift is real and measurable. TestGorilla's annual survey found skills-based hiring climbed from 56% of employers in 2022 to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% in 2025</a>, while reliance on résumés fell over the same period. It is worth staying honest about this, because adoption is sometimes claimed more loudly than it is practiced. But the trend line is not ambiguous, and it is moving in the direction of evidence.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why are companies moving away from degree-first hiring?</h2>
      <p>Because the degree stopped predicting performance well. A credential was always a proxy, a way to guess at ability the employer could not measure directly, and that proxy has weakened as the work has changed faster than any curriculum can follow. The result shows up in the outcomes: about <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>, working jobs that do not require their degree, which tells employers the credential is no longer a reliable signal of job-readiness. When the proxy breaks, smart employers stop trusting it and start asking for evidence instead.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What does this mean for a student with no experience?</h2>
      <p>It is genuinely good news, and most students miss why. Degree-first hiring is a game you mostly cannot win as a young applicant, because pedigree is fixed by the time you are applying and the most prestigious credentials went to people sorted years earlier. Performance-based hiring reopens the contest. It rewards what you can produce right now, and producing good work is the one area where a motivated student with no title can match or beat a more credentialed competitor.</p>
      <p>The catch is the part students underestimate. Performance-based hiring rewards demonstrated skill, but it only works if the skill is demonstrated. An employer cannot reward work they never see. So the job is not just to be capable, it is to make your capability visible and verifiable, in a form a recruiter can evaluate quickly. Our guide on <a href="/learn/what-do-recruiters-look-at-when-a-student-has-no-work-experience">what recruiters look at when you have no experience</a> goes deeper on what that signal looks like.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you prepare for performance-based evaluation?</h2>
      <p>Treat preparation as production. Pick the role you want, do the real work of it on a few scoped problems, and get each piece evaluated against a professional standard so you know where it stands. That way, when an employer runs a skills test or asks for a work sample, you are not starting from zero, you already have evaluated proof. This is what zero is built for: you take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against the bar strong professionals are held to, and the scored result becomes a receipt a recruiter can evaluate, while recruiters pay for access to that talent so it stays free for you. Performance-based hiring asks for evidence. The preparation is simply to have it ready.</p>
    </section>

